WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:02.540
Film Courage: Your first short film was in
1994?

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:04.220
Adisa, Filmmaker: Yes.

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:09.830
It was called NOTES IN A MINOR KEY and my
collaborator on that was Avery Williams.

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:14.440
It was more like a mini feature.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:19.490
I say that because Avery (I love him dearly.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:20.970
He’s in Atlanta right now.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:28.199
He’s teaching at Morehouse) but he wrote
that script and he brought it to me to direct.

00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:31.939
And I was like Well are we going to do this
in school?

00:00:31.939 --> 00:00:34.320
And we sort of did do it in school but we
sort of didn’t do it.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.160
We kind of broke all of the rules right?

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:46.180
We used the school’s equipment and we started
the film’s process there but then because

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:50.780
of some of my earlier work I had gotten some
attention from Disney, Hollywood Pictures

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:57.879
and they had started this discretionary fund
program and so they decided to give us $40,000

00:00:57.879 --> 00:00:58.879
to make the movie.

00:00:58.879 --> 00:01:02.880
It was a period film, period film, set in
Harlem in 1947.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:07.530
And we had Keith David in it, we had Harry
Lennix in it (both big actors right now).

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.009
We were just young kids.

00:01:10.009 --> 00:01:13.409
I was just fresh behind the ears (21 - 22).

00:01:13.409 --> 00:01:17.619
But you know not really knowing a whole lot
about what we’re doing, figuring it out

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:24.079
as we go and keeping it very professional
That film went on to get a lot of awards and

00:01:24.079 --> 00:01:27.679
get both of us a lot of attention.

00:01:27.679 --> 00:01:30.819
Film Courage: And $40,000 in 1994?

00:01:30.819 --> 00:01:31.819
That’s a lot.

00:01:31.819 --> 00:01:32.819
Adisa: That’s a lot.

00:01:32.819 --> 00:01:37.469
Film Courage: It’s 2019 now and you are
just releasing your first feature film?

00:01:37.469 --> 00:01:39.039
Adisa: Yes.

00:01:39.039 --> 00:01:43.340
I think this old adage about what’s holding
you back?

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:44.340
You?

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:45.700
It probably stands true.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.329
I think for a years I resisted writing.

00:01:48.329 --> 00:01:51.999
I became a better writer over the years.

00:01:51.999 --> 00:01:56.539
But when I first started out I didn’t particularly
write as much.

00:01:56.539 --> 00:02:02.570
I think the filmmakers that are really prolific
are the ones that can get there faster, they

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:05.340
generate their own material and they write.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.809
So it goes hand in hand, they write and the
direct.

00:02:07.809 --> 00:02:13.269
If you don’t write you are going to either
have to find material or work with other writers.

00:02:13.269 --> 00:02:15.549
I didn’t write for a lot of years.

00:02:15.549 --> 00:02:19.129
When you look at the gap from when I graduated
film school to when I came out with my first

00:02:19.129 --> 00:02:27.440
feature, a lot of it was because I was learning
how to write in that period of time.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:31.090
Film Courage: Were you reading screenplay
books?

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:35.709
Adisa: I was reading screenplay books but
then life happens.

00:02:35.709 --> 00:02:44.760
So I came out here in early 2,000 with high
hopes of directing but it just didn’t happen

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:45.760
to me right away.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.629
It didn’t happen and I had to get a job
and then I started looking at what are my

00:02:49.629 --> 00:02:50.629
options?

00:02:50.629 --> 00:02:52.010
I’d always done sound in school.

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:59.709
I was a pretty good sound person in school
so I started doing sound out in LA for commercials

00:02:59.709 --> 00:03:01.129
and videos.

00:03:01.129 --> 00:03:08.050
Film Courage: What was it that made you want
to do a feature film?

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:11.090
Why were you ready, what was it two years
ago?

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:16.520
Adisa: Time was getting away from me and that
was my dream.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:20.590
I didn’t want to get away from my dream
of being a director and so I just hankered

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:22.530
down and did whatever it took.

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:23.530
That was my mantra.

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:27.310
I just decided I’m going to do whatever
it takes and I’m going to confront myself

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:32.019
at every corner and look at my weaknesses
and I’m going to make my weaknesses into

00:03:32.019 --> 00:03:33.019
strengths.

00:03:33.019 --> 00:03:38.760
So then I just started to write and write
and write.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:46.099
Then I hooked up with another collaborator/writer
Steven Palmer Peterson and he wrote SKIN IN

00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:47.540
THE GAME.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:53.290
Film Courage: So were you almost emulating
Spike Lee, getting up at 6:00 a.m.

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:56.569
Adisa: I was getting up at 6:00 a.m.

00:03:56.569 --> 00:04:02.719
Even right now, it takes a lot to be a filmmaker
and I think those that make it are probably

00:04:02.719 --> 00:04:08.690
more determination and persistence versus
talent a lot of times I mean talent hand-in-hand

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:13.010
(you don’t want to take that for granted)
but you want to be talented at it.

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:17.419
I think it’s really about persistence and
determination and how much you’re willing

00:04:17.419 --> 00:04:21.079
to sacrifice in some cases.

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:29.490
Because in some cases you may not be eating,
you may be eating Top Ramen or taking some

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:33.000
least attractive jobs because it allows more
time to write.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.729
And I think I made that decision and you’re
not leaning on other people.

00:04:37.729 --> 00:04:43.000
In other words the responsibility for your
life and becoming a filmmaker is yours.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.781
It doesn’t belong to your wife or your partners
or your boyfriends or your mother’s or your

00:04:46.781 --> 00:04:47.781
father’s.

00:04:47.781 --> 00:04:50.750
So it’s not about them sponsoring you or
giving you a cushion or saying “Okay, you

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:51.750
go ahead and write.

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:54.520
I’m going to go ahead and pay your rent
for the next couple of months.”

00:04:54.520 --> 00:05:00.710
So it was about taking complete responsibility
for my dream and what I wanted to make happen

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:02.979
and what that looked like.

00:05:02.979 --> 00:05:06.830
So if it meant working all day and writing
all night, that’s what I did.

